+## Design Notes
+
+The defect pattern was "SSR renders disabled, JS enables" — a progressive *requirement*. The correct inversion needs no new JS: form.js's GDPR-consent IIFE runs `refresh()` at init (deferred script, DOM ready), which sets `data-consent-blocked` when the checkbox is unticked and the shared recompute sets `submit.disabled = true`. Removing the SSR attribute therefore leaves the JS-enabled UX pixel-identical (brief enabled flash pre-defer is harmless — the server rejects an unticked-consent POST anyway), while no-JS clients get a working submit whose enforcement lives entirely server-side. Serialized Lustre renders attributes sorted by name with empty-valued ones bare, so the bug renders as `data-testid="submit-application" disabled type="submit"` — the pin probes exactly that window.
+
